romoted through the Federalist Papers, written by Alexander Hamilton, James Madison, and John Jay."}, {"sourceSlug": "united-states-constitution", "sourceName": "United States Constitution", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "civil-rights-movement", "targetName": "Civil Rights Movement", "context": "The ideals of the United States Constitution transmitted to the Civil Rights Movement, influencing leaders like Martin Luther King Jr. in the 1960s."}], "places": [{"name": "Philadelphia, United States", "role": "Drafting Site"}, {"name": "Washington, D.C., United States", "role": "Seat of Government"}, {"name": "New York City, United States", "role": "First Capital"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: United States Constitution", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "united-states-constitution"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q11698", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitution_of_the_United_States"], "tags": ["constitution", "Modern Constitutions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "United States Constitution left a notable mark on Early Modern history, documented across 122 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The United States Constitution established a framework for government that has endured for over two centuries, influencing democratic principles worldwide and leading to significant changes in civil rights and governance.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-08T01:35:04Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27111092777", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Supreme law of the united states of america since march 4th, 1789 Created by Constitutional Convention. Created c. 1787.", "newValue": "The United States Constitution, ratified on June 21, 1788 and implemented on March 4, 1789, serves as the foundational legal document of the United States, establishing the framework of the national government and delineating the rights of citizens. Drafted during the Constitutional Convention in Philadelphia, it was primarily authored by James Madison, who is often referred to as the 'Father of the Constitution.' This document not only marked the beginning of a new era in governance but also influenced democratic movements worldwide, symbolizing the emergence of a republic based on the principles of liberty and justice.

The Constitution arose from a critical need to replace the Articles of Confederation, which had proven inadequate in unifying the states and managing national affairs. The Constitutional Convention brought together 55 delegates, including key figures like George Washington and Benjamin Franklin, who debated and crafted a framework that included the separation of powers, checks and balances, and the Bill of Rights, ratified in 1791. The document has been amended 27 times to adapt to changing societal needs, reflecting its foundational role in American law and governance.

